* LRA characterisation -- and a hard requirement it uncovered.
*
* WHY LRA AT ALL.  CP's TRANS macro is invoked 174 times and its core is
*
*     LCTL  C1,C1,VMSEG -  GET SEGMENT TABLE ORIGIN
*     LRA   &RV,0(0,&UR)   AND DO HARDWARE TRANSLATE
*     BC    8,TRN&NL       PAGE IS RESIDENT
*
* so every virtual-to-real translation in CP goes through one LRA and a
* condition-code test.  Appendix F of the ESA/390 Principles of Operation
* lists "Changes to LOAD REAL ADDRESS" among the differences from S/370, and
* until this test those 174 sites rested on assumed behaviour.
*
* WHAT IT FOUND, WHICH IS NOT WHAT IT WAS LOOKING FOR
* ---------------------------------------------------
* The worry was LRA's RESULT: if it truncated to 24 bits in 24-bit mode,
* TRANS could never return an above-the-line real address.  The result is
* fine -- control.c caps at 2 GB, not 16 MB, and the addressing mode does
* not enter into it.
*
* BUT THE OPERAND ADDRESS IS TRUNCATED, AND THAT IS WORSE.
*
*     24-bit mode:  LRA 2,0(0,6)  R6=01005000  ->  cc 0, R2 = 00005000
*     31-bit mode:  LRA 2,0(0,6)  R6=01005000  ->  cc 0, R2 = 01100000
*
* In 24-bit mode the effective address is masked to 005000 BEFORE
* translation.  LRA is asked about segment 0 page 5, which is validly
* mapped, and answers correctly about the wrong address.  Condition code 0,
* a real address that looks entirely reasonable, and the wrong page.
*
* THE REQUIREMENT THIS IMPOSES.  While CP runs AMODE 24, its 174 TRANS
* sites CANNOT ASK ABOUT AN ABOVE-THE-LINE VIRTUAL ADDRESS AT ALL -- the
* question is truncated before it is put, silently and plausibly.
*
* So converting the modules containing TRANS to AMODE 31 is a PREREQUISITE
* for paging above the line, not an independent choice that can be deferred
* to a later milestone.
*
* A NOTE ON TEST DESIGN.  A version of case A that checked only cc=0 would
* have PASSED, because cc=0 is exactly what a truncated-but-valid
* translation returns.  Comparing the returned real address against the
* expected one is what caught it.  Worth remembering for the rest of this
* series.
*
* HOW TO READ THE RESULT
*   006009   pass, all five cases.
*   000F01   case A: cc was not 0 in 24-bit mode.
*   000F02   case A: the 24-bit answer was not the truncated one.  Read R2 --
*            if it is 01100000 then this build does NOT truncate the operand,
*            which would be a genuine difference from the one measured here.
*   000F11   case A2: cc was not 0 in 31-bit mode.
*   000F12   case A2: R2 was not 01100000.  Read R2.
*   000F03   case B: STE invalid did not give cc=1.
*   000F04   case C: PTE invalid did not give cc=2.
*   000F05   case D: beyond segment-table length did not give cc=3.
*   000DED   program check -- read the old PSW at X'150'.
*
* FIVE CASES.  A runs 24-bit; the rest need 31-bit, because their virtual
* addresses are above the line and would otherwise truncate into a
* different and validly-mapped part of the address space.
*
*   A   cc 0  24-bit: operand truncated, answer for the wrong page
*   A2  cc 0  31-bit: same instruction, real 01100000
*   B   cc 1  segment-table entry invalid          (segment 1)
*   C   cc 2  page-table entry invalid             (segment 16 page 6)
*   D   cc 3  beyond the segment-table length      (segment 32, STL 1)
*
* DAT IS OFF THROUGHOUT.  LRA translates through the tables designated by
* CR1 whether or not DAT is enabled in the PSW, and CP relies on that.
*
* A FIXTURE CORRECTION.  dat4b.py fills unused page-table entries with
* X'00000020' -- that is the SEGMENT invalid bit.  In a PAGE table entry the
* invalid bit is X'00000400', so those entries are actually VALID with a
* frame address of zero.  Harmless to 4b, which only referenced page 5, but
* case C here needs a real invalid PTE and builds one with X'400'.
*
* TABLES   SEGTAB 3000 (32 entries, STL 1)  PAGETAB0 3080  PAGETAB16 30C0
*          SEGTAB[0]=3080  SEGTAB[1]=20 invalid  SEGTAB[16]=30C0
*          PAGETAB16[5]=01100000   PAGETAB16[6]=400 invalid
*
* Built by build/lra1.py.
*
